/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-106 NIST Controls Baseline */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-106"] = {
ov:"The NIST SP 800-53 control catalog is the vocabulary of federal security, and writing implementation statements that survive audit is a defining skill of the ISSO. This course navigates the catalog's structure and major families, then teaches you to document how a system actually satisfies each control — the difference between a package that passes and one that generates findings. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"The Structure of SP 800-53",d:"Catalog, families, controls, enhancements, and parameters.",
obj:["Describe the hierarchical structure of the 800-53 catalog","Explain controls, enhancements, and organization-defined parameters","Explain how the catalog relates to baselines and tailoring"],
read:["SP 800-53 is a catalog of security and privacy controls organized hierarchically. Controls are grouped into families identified by two-letter prefixes (AC for Access Control, AU for Audit and Accountability, and so on). Within a family, individual controls (AC-2, Account Management) state a security capability, and many controls have enhancements (AC-2(1), AC-2(3)) that add stricter or additional requirements layered on the base control. Understanding this structure — family, control, enhancement — is basic literacy for reading any baseline or SSP.",
"Controls also contain organization-defined parameters: bracketed values the catalog deliberately leaves for the implementing organization to set (the frequency of a review, the length of a timeout, the threshold for lockout). This is the parameter-assignment tailoring from BC-105 seen from the catalog side — the control specifies what must be decided, and the organization supplies the value based on its risk tolerance. A control statement is incomplete until its parameters are filled with the actual values the system uses.",
"The catalog is the source; baselines (SP 800-53B) are curated selections from it by impact level, and tailoring adjusts that selection to the system (BC-105). Rev.5 restructured the catalog to be outcome-based and consolidated privacy controls into it. When you work a package, you are constantly moving between the catalog (what the control requires), the baseline (which controls apply at your impact level), and the SSP (how your system satisfies each). Holding the structure clearly is what keeps that navigation from becoming confusion."],
ex:"An ISSO reading a Moderate baseline encounters AC-2 (Account Management) with enhancements AC-2(1) through AC-2(3) applicable, and notes AC-2 contains a parameter for how often inactive accounts are disabled. They set it to the organization's value (e.g., 35 days) and document how the system satisfies the base control and each enhancement — moving fluently between catalog, baseline, and SSP because they understand the structure.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, Ch.2","The control structure: families, controls, enhancements, and parameters."],["NIST SP 800-53B","How baselines select controls from the catalog by impact."]],
kc:[
{q:"In SP 800-53, a control enhancement (e.g., AC-2(1)) is:",o:["A separate unrelated control","A stricter or additional requirement layered on a base control","A deleted control","A vendor product"],a:1,x:"Enhancements build on a base control, adding rigor or additional requirements — AC-2(1) strengthens the AC-2 account-management control."},
{q:"An organization-defined parameter in a control is:",o:["A fixed value NIST sets","A bracketed value the implementing organization supplies based on its risk tolerance (e.g., a review frequency)","An assessment result","A network setting only"],a:1,x:"The catalog deliberately leaves certain values for the organization to define; a control statement is incomplete until these are set."},
{q:"The relationship between the catalog, baselines, and the SSP is:",o:["They are the same document","The catalog is the source, baselines select controls by impact, and the SSP documents how the system satisfies each","Baselines replace the catalog","The SSP defines the catalog"],a:1,x:"You move between catalog (requirement), baseline (applicability), and SSP (implementation) — understanding the structure enables that navigation."}]},
{t:"Control Families Overview",d:"The full set of families and what each governs.",
obj:["Identify the major control families by prefix and purpose","Group families into functional categories","Explain why the family structure aids organization and inheritance"],
read:["SP 800-53 organizes controls into around twenty families, each addressing a domain. Technical families include Access Control (AC), Identification and Authentication (IA), System and Communications Protection (SC), Audit and Accountability (AU), and System and Information Integrity (SI). Operational families include Configuration Management (CM), Incident Response (IR), Contingency Planning (CP), Maintenance (MA), and Physical and Environmental Protection (PE). Management families include Risk Assessment (RA), Security Assessment and Authorization (CA), Planning (PL), and Program Management (PM). Rev.5 added Supply Chain Risk Management (SR) and integrated Privacy (PT).",
"Knowing a family's prefix and purpose lets you place any control instantly: a control starting with IR concerns incident response, one starting with SC concerns communications protection. This matters constantly in practice — assessors, scanners, and packages all reference controls by identifier, and fluency with the families turns cryptic labels into meaning. The families also map roughly to the functional and implementation-class taxonomy from BC-101 (technical, operational, management), giving you two lenses on the same control set.",
"The family structure also organizes inheritance and division of labor. Some families (like PE, physical security) are typically inherited as common controls from the organization or hosting provider; others (like SI, system integrity) are usually system-specific. Grouping controls by family lets a program assign responsibility coherently — the facilities team owns PE, the SOC owns much of IR and SI, the engineering team owns SC and CM — connecting the control catalog to the governance and role assignments of the earlier courses."],
ex:"Reviewing a scanner report that flags a deficiency in CM-6 and AU-6, an ISSO immediately knows these concern Configuration Management (secure configuration settings) and Audit and Accountability (audit review), assigns them to the engineering and SOC teams respectively, and notes that the related PE controls are inherited from the data-center provider. Family fluency turned identifiers into an action plan.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, control family listing","The full set of families, prefixes, and their scope."],["NIST SP 800-53B baselines","How controls across families populate the impact baselines."]],
kc:[
{q:"The two-letter prefix 'IR' identifies which control family?",o:["Identity Records","Incident Response","Internal Routing","Information Rights"],a:1,x:"IR is the Incident Response family, governing detection, handling, and reporting of incidents."},
{q:"Physical and Environmental (PE) controls are, in many systems:",o:["Always system-specific","Typically inherited as common controls from the organization or hosting provider","Never required","Assessed by the vendor only"],a:1,x:"Facility physical security is usually provided once at the organizational/provider level and inherited, per BC-105's common-control concept."},
{q:"Fluency with control-family prefixes primarily helps you:",o:["Write code faster","Instantly interpret and assign any control referenced by identifier in packages, scans, and assessments","Avoid using the catalog","Skip the SSP"],a:1,x:"Assessors and tools reference controls by ID; knowing families turns cryptic labels into meaning and enables coherent responsibility assignment."}]},
{t:"Access Control (AC) Family",d:"Accounts, least privilege, and access enforcement.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the AC family","Identify key AC controls and their intent","Connect AC controls to IAM concepts from BC-104"],
read:["The Access Control family governs who can access the system and what they can do — the catalog expression of the IAM discipline from BC-104. Cornerstone controls include AC-2 (Account Management: creating, modifying, disabling, and reviewing accounts — the joiner-mover-leaver lifecycle), AC-3 (Access Enforcement: the mechanism that actually enforces authorization decisions), AC-5 (Separation of Duties), AC-6 (Least Privilege), and AC-17 (Remote Access). These controls, properly implemented, are the technical backbone of authorization.",
"AC controls are where least privilege and separation of duties become auditable requirements rather than principles. AC-6 (Least Privilege) and its enhancements demand that the system restrict privileges to the minimum necessary and review them; AC-2 demands disciplined account lifecycle management including prompt disabling of departed users. An assessor testing these controls looks for exactly the failures BC-104 catalogued — orphaned accounts, privilege creep, missing recertification — so implementing AC well means operationalizing the IAM lessons.",
"Because access control is so central, the AC family is large and heavily weighted in baselines, and its controls interconnect with IA (authentication proves the identity that AC authorizes) and AU (audit records what access occurred). A well-documented AC implementation traces directly to concrete mechanisms — the directory's account management, the RBAC roles, the MFA enforcement, the access reviews — and this is where the identity architecture of BC-104 must show up as specific, verifiable control statements in the SSP."],
ex:"An ISSO documents AC-2 by describing the system's automated joiner-mover-leaver integration with HR, the quarterly access recertification process, and the automatic disabling of accounts after 35 days of inactivity. The statement names the mechanism (the identity governance platform), the process, and the parameter — a verifiable implementation that an assessor can test, directly reflecting BC-104's lifecycle discipline.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC family","Account management, access enforcement, least privilege, and separation of duties controls."],["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC-6 and AC-2","The specific least-privilege and account-management requirements."]],
kc:[
{q:"AC-2 (Account Management) requires disciplined handling of:",o:["Network cables","Account creation, modification, disabling, and review — the identity lifecycle","Firewall rules only","Physical badges"],a:1,x:"AC-2 operationalizes the joiner-mover-leaver lifecycle as an auditable control, including prompt disabling of departed users."},
{q:"AC-6 (Least Privilege) requires the system to:",o:["Grant maximum access for convenience","Restrict privileges to the minimum necessary and review them","Disable all accounts","Encrypt the disk"],a:1,x:"AC-6 makes least privilege an auditable requirement — assessors look for privilege creep and excessive standing access."},
{q:"The AC family interconnects most directly with which families?",o:["PE and MA only","IA (authentication proves the identity AC authorizes) and AU (audit records access)","No other families","SR only"],a:1,x:"Access control depends on authentication (IA) to establish identity and audit (AU) to record access — the three work together."}]},
{t:"Audit and Accountability (AU) Family",d:"Logging, review, and non-repudiation.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the AU family","Identify key AU controls and their intent","Connect AU controls to logging practices from earlier courses"],
read:["The Audit and Accountability family ensures that security-relevant events are recorded, protected, reviewed, and attributable — the catalog expression of the logging discipline from BC-102 and BC-103. Key controls include AU-2 (Event Logging: deciding what to log), AU-3 (Content of Audit Records: ensuring records contain enough detail — who, what, when, where, outcome), AU-6 (Audit Review, Analysis, and Reporting: actually examining the logs), AU-9 (Protection of Audit Information: tamper resistance), and AU-11 (Retention).",
"These controls encode lessons the earlier courses taught operationally. AU-2 and AU-3 are the deliberate-audit-configuration point (Windows audit policy, Linux auditd) that determines whether logs are useful. AU-6 is the anti-alert-fatigue requirement — logs unreviewed are logs wasted, the retailer-breach lesson from BC-102. AU-9 is central log protection and tamper resistance — the forwarding that defeats log-clearing from BC-103. The AU family makes these practices auditable requirements rather than good ideas.",
"Accountability is the family's deeper purpose: audit records must attribute actions to individuals (which is why shared accounts break accounting, per BC-104) and be protected so they can be trusted as evidence, supporting non-repudiation. A strong AU implementation traces to the actual logging architecture — what events are captured, how they are centrally collected and protected, who reviews them and how often, and how long they are retained — turning the SIEM and log-management reality into verifiable control statements the assessor can examine."],
ex:"An ISSO documents AU-6 by describing the SIEM that aggregates forwarded logs, the daily analyst review process, the automated alerting on high-fidelity events, and the monthly reporting to the ISSM. This names the mechanism and the process, showing the logs are not merely collected but actually reviewed — satisfying the control in a way that the untuned, ignored console of the BC-102 breach story would have failed.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AU family","Event logging, audit content, review, protection, and retention controls."],["NIST SP 800-92","Log management practices that AU controls formalize."]],
kc:[
{q:"AU-6 (Audit Review, Analysis, and Reporting) exists because:",o:["Logs must be encrypted","Logs that are collected but never reviewed provide no detection value","Retention is unlimited","Audit is optional"],a:1,x:"AU-6 requires actually examining logs — the anti-alert-fatigue requirement; unreviewed logs are wasted, as the retailer-breach lesson showed."},
{q:"AU-9 (Protection of Audit Information) is satisfied in part by:",o:["Deleting logs frequently","Central collection/forwarding and tamper resistance so logs survive attacker attempts to clear them","Storing logs only locally","Disabling logging"],a:1,x:"Protecting audit information means it cannot be easily altered or erased — central forwarding defeats local log-clearing."},
{q:"The AU family supports accountability, which requires audit records to be:",o:["Anonymous","Attributable to individuals and protected so they can be trusted as evidence","Deleted after a day","Shared publicly"],a:1,x:"Accountability and non-repudiation depend on attributable, tamper-protected records — why shared accounts undermine the AU family's purpose."}]},
{t:"Configuration Management (CM) Family",d:"Baselines, change control, and least functionality.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the CM family","Identify key CM controls and their intent","Connect CM controls to hardening and change management"],
read:["The Configuration Management family ensures systems are securely configured and that changes are controlled — the catalog expression of the hardening discipline from BC-103 and the change management this program treats as central. Key controls include CM-2 (Baseline Configuration: a documented, secure baseline), CM-6 (Configuration Settings: enforcing secure settings, the CIS/STIG hardening), CM-7 (Least Functionality: disabling unnecessary services and ports), CM-3 (Configuration Change Control: reviewing and approving changes), and CM-8 (System Component Inventory).",
"CM-6 and CM-7 are where the hardening baselines of BC-103 become auditable requirements — the system must enforce secure configuration settings and run only necessary functionality, and an assessor checks compliance against the documented baseline. CM-2 requires that a secure baseline configuration exist and be maintained, and CM-8 requires an accurate component inventory, without which you cannot know what you are protecting (a recurring theme). These controls operationalize least functionality and secure configuration as requirements, not aspirations.",
"CM-3 (Change Control) connects to the significant-versus-minor change decision that recurs throughout this program: changes must be reviewed for security impact and approved through a controlled process, so that the secure baseline does not drift and undocumented changes do not silently invalidate the authorization (BC-101's paper-mirroring warning). A strong CM implementation traces to the actual configuration-management tooling, the change advisory process, and the enforced baselines — the difference between a system that stays hardened and one that decays into an inaccurate SSP."],
ex:"An ISSO documents CM-6 by describing the CIS-benchmark baseline enforced via configuration-as-code, the automated compliance scanning that detects drift, and the documented exceptions. CM-3 is documented via the change advisory board that reviews changes for security impact before approval. Together these show the system stays in a known, hardened, controlled state — the operational reality behind the control statements.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, CM family","Baseline configuration, settings, least functionality, change control, and inventory controls."],["NIST SP 800-128","Security-focused configuration management of information systems."]],
kc:[
{q:"CM-7 (Least Functionality) requires the system to:",o:["Run every available service","Disable unnecessary services, ports, and functions to reduce attack surface","Encrypt all traffic","Log every event"],a:1,x:"CM-7 makes the least-functionality hardening principle from BC-103 an auditable requirement — run only what is needed."},
{q:"CM-3 (Configuration Change Control) prevents which failure?",o:["Weak passwords","Undocumented changes that cause baseline drift and silently invalidate the authorization","Network outages","Phishing"],a:1,x:"Controlled, security-reviewed change management keeps the system matching its SSP, avoiding the paper-mirroring drift BC-101 warned of."},
{q:"CM-8 (System Component Inventory) is foundational because:",o:["It is required for billing","You cannot secure or assess what you have not inventoried","It replaces the SSP","It encrypts components"],a:1,x:"An accurate inventory underpins nearly every other control — you cannot protect, patch, or assess unknown components."}]},
{t:"Identification and Authentication (IA) Family",d:"Proving identity to the system.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the IA family","Identify key IA controls and their intent","Connect IA controls to authentication concepts from BC-104"],
read:["The Identification and Authentication family governs how users and devices prove their identity — the catalog expression of the authentication discipline from BC-104. Key controls include IA-2 (Identification and Authentication of organizational users, including the MFA requirements), IA-5 (Authenticator Management: password/credential policies, including strength and lifecycle), IA-3 (Device Identification and Authentication), and IA-8 (Non-organizational users). IA-2 enhancements specifically mandate multi-factor authentication for privileged and network access, encoding the MFA lessons as requirements.",
"IA-5 (Authenticator Management) is where BC-104's password and credential guidance becomes auditable: it governs how authenticators are established, distributed, stored (hashed, not plaintext), rotated, and revoked. The modern NIST password guidance from BC-104 lives here, and an assessor checks that credential handling meets it. IA-2's MFA enhancements are increasingly central as the field moves toward phishing-resistant authentication — a Moderate or High system's baseline mandates MFA, and the implementation must show which method and where.",
"The IA family works hand-in-glove with AC: IA establishes and verifies the identity that AC then authorizes. A strong IA implementation traces to the actual authentication architecture from BC-104 — the identity provider, the MFA method and its coverage, the credential lifecycle, the phishing-resistance posture for privileged accounts. Documenting IA well means translating that architecture into specific control statements: not the system uses authentication but the system enforces phishing-resistant MFA for all privileged access via [named mechanism], with credentials managed per [policy]."],
ex:"An ISSO documents IA-2(1) by stating that all privileged access requires FIDO2 phishing-resistant MFA enforced by the identity provider, and IA-5 by describing the credential lifecycle — breach-screened passphrases, hashed storage with Argon2, and automated deprovisioning. The statements name mechanisms and are verifiable, reflecting BC-104's phishing-resistant and storage guidance as concrete, assessable controls.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, IA family","Identification, authentication, MFA, and authenticator-management controls."],["NIST SP 800-63B","The digital authentication requirements that IA-2 and IA-5 reference."]],
kc:[
{q:"IA-2 enhancements specifically mandate:",o:["Disk encryption","Multi-factor authentication for privileged and network access","Physical locks","Log retention"],a:1,x:"IA-2's enhancements encode the MFA requirement from BC-104 as an auditable control for privileged and network access."},
{q:"IA-5 (Authenticator Management) governs:",o:["Firewall rules","How authenticators (passwords, credentials) are established, stored, rotated, and revoked","Network segmentation","Physical badges only"],a:1,x:"IA-5 is where BC-104's credential guidance — hashed storage, breach screening, lifecycle — becomes an auditable requirement."},
{q:"The IA family relates to the AC family in that IA:",o:["Replaces AC","Establishes and verifies the identity that AC then authorizes","Is unrelated to AC","Only covers devices"],a:1,x:"Authentication (IA) proves who a subject is; access control (AC) then decides what that verified identity may do."}]},
{t:"System and Communications Protection (SC) Family",d:"Boundaries, cryptography, and data-in-transit protection.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the SC family","Identify key SC controls and their intent","Connect SC controls to network security and cryptography"],
read:["The System and Communications Protection family governs the protection of data in transit and the boundaries between system components — the catalog expression of the network security from BC-102 and the cryptography of IC-206. Key controls include SC-7 (Boundary Protection: firewalls, segmentation, and controlling the flow of information across boundaries), SC-8 (Transmission Confidentiality and Integrity: encrypting data in transit), SC-13 (Cryptographic Protection: using approved, validated cryptography), and SC-28 (Protection of Information at Rest).",
"SC-7 (Boundary Protection) is where the segmentation lessons from BC-102 become an auditable requirement — the system must control communications at its external boundary and key internal boundaries, implementing the defense-in-depth and segmentation that limit blast radius. SC-8 and SC-13 require that data in transit be encrypted using FIPS-validated cryptography, encoding the TLS and cryptographic discipline; SC-28 extends protection to data at rest (the disk encryption of BC-103). These controls make protecting data in motion and at rest, with proper cryptography, a requirement.",
"The emphasis on validated cryptography (SC-13) matters: federal systems must use FIPS-validated cryptographic modules, not merely encryption. An assessor checks that the algorithms and modules are approved and correctly configured — strong encryption implemented with an unvalidated or misconfigured module is a finding. A strong SC implementation traces to the actual boundary architecture (the segmentation from BC-102, the firewalls, the flow controls) and the cryptographic configuration (TLS versions, validated modules), translating network and crypto engineering into verifiable control statements."],
ex:"An ISSO documents SC-7 by describing the enterprise boundary firewall (inherited common control) plus internal micro-segmentation isolating the database tier, and SC-8/SC-13 by specifying TLS 1.2+ with FIPS-validated modules for all data in transit. The statements name the mechanisms and the validated cryptography, giving the assessor concrete, testable claims — the network and crypto architecture expressed as controls.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, SC family","Boundary protection, transmission protection, and cryptographic controls."],["FIPS 140-3","The validation standard for cryptographic modules that SC-13 requires."]],
kc:[
{q:"SC-7 (Boundary Protection) makes which BC-102 concept an auditable requirement?",o:["Password strength","Controlling communications at boundaries — firewalls and segmentation that limit blast radius","Disk encryption","Log review"],a:1,x:"SC-7 requires boundary and flow control, encoding the segmentation and defense-in-depth that contain lateral movement."},
{q:"SC-13 (Cryptographic Protection) for federal systems requires:",o:["Any encryption at all","FIPS-validated cryptographic modules, correctly configured","Only physical security","No cryptography"],a:1,x:"Federal systems must use validated modules; strong encryption with an unvalidated or misconfigured module is a finding."},
{q:"SC-8 and SC-28 together protect data:",o:["Only on paper","In transit (SC-8) and at rest (SC-28), respectively","Only during backups","Only in the cloud"],a:1,x:"SC-8 covers transmission confidentiality/integrity and SC-28 covers information at rest — protecting data in both states."}]},
{t:"System and Information Integrity (SI) Family",d:"Flaw remediation, malware defense, and monitoring.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the SI family","Identify key SI controls and their intent","Connect SI controls to patching, endpoint defense, and monitoring"],
read:["The System and Information Integrity family ensures the system detects and responds to flaws, malicious code, and integrity violations — the catalog expression of the patching, endpoint defense, and monitoring from earlier courses. Key controls include SI-2 (Flaw Remediation: timely patching), SI-3 (Malicious Code Protection: antivirus/EDR), SI-4 (System Monitoring: intrusion detection and security monitoring), and SI-7 (Software, Firmware, and Information Integrity: detecting unauthorized changes).",
"SI-2 (Flaw Remediation) makes patch management from BC-103 an auditable requirement — the system must identify, report, and correct flaws within defined timeframes, encoding the patch-latency discipline. SI-3 requires malicious-code protection (the EDR/antivirus of BC-103). SI-4 (System Monitoring) makes the detection discipline from BC-102 a requirement — the system must be monitored for attacks and indicators, connecting to IDS/IPS and the SIEM. These controls turn the operational security practices into assessable obligations with defined parameters (patch timeframes, monitoring scope).",
"SI-4 is one of the most consequential controls because it mandates the detection capability that everything after prevention depends on (BC-101's resilience argument). An assessor testing SI-4 looks for actual monitoring coverage, alerting, and response — not just a tool present but a functioning detection process. A strong SI implementation traces to the real patch-management SLAs, the EDR deployment and coverage, and the monitoring architecture, translating BC-102's and BC-103's operational defenses into control statements that show the system can find and fix problems, not merely try to prevent them."],
ex:"An ISSO documents SI-2 with the severity-based patch SLAs and the vulnerability-management tooling that tracks remediation, SI-3 with the EDR deployment and its coverage percentage, and SI-4 with the SIEM, its monitoring scope, and the SOC response process. The statements show measurable detection and remediation capability — the resilience-oriented controls that assume prevention will sometimes fail.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, SI family","Flaw remediation, malicious code protection, monitoring, and integrity controls."],["NIST SP 800-40 Rev.4","Patch management practices that SI-2 formalizes."]],
kc:[
{q:"SI-2 (Flaw Remediation) makes which practice an auditable requirement?",o:["Password rotation","Timely patching within defined timeframes — the patch-latency discipline","Physical security","Log encryption"],a:1,x:"SI-2 requires identifying and correcting flaws within defined periods, encoding patch management as a measurable control."},
{q:"SI-4 (System Monitoring) is especially consequential because it mandates:",o:["Encryption at rest","The detection capability that resilience depends on once prevention fails","Faster networking","Physical guards"],a:1,x:"SI-4 requires functioning security monitoring — the detection that BC-101 argued is essential because prevention eventually fails."},
{q:"An assessor testing SI-4 looks for:",o:["A monitoring tool merely being installed","Actual monitoring coverage, alerting, and a functioning response process","The number of firewalls","The password policy"],a:1,x:"SI-4 requires effective monitoring, not just tool presence — coverage, alerting, and response must actually work."}]},
{t:"Operational Families: IR, CP, PE, MA",d:"Incident response, contingency, physical, and maintenance controls.",
obj:["Describe the scope of the IR, CP, PE, and MA families","Identify key controls in each and their intent","Explain which of these are commonly inherited"],
read:["Beyond the core technical families, several operational families govern how the organization runs and protects the system day to day. The Incident Response (IR) family requires an incident response capability — IR-4 (Incident Handling), IR-6 (Incident Reporting), IR-8 (Incident Response Plan) — encoding the IR discipline of BC-110 as auditable requirements. The Contingency Planning (CP) family requires the ability to recover — CP-2 (Contingency Plan), CP-9 (System Backup), CP-10 (Recovery) — making the business continuity and backup concepts from BC-101 into controls with defined RTO/RPO parameters.",
"The Physical and Environmental Protection (PE) family protects the physical system — PE-2/PE-3 (physical access authorization and control), PE-13 (fire protection), PE-14 (environmental controls). In cloud and data-center environments these are typically inherited common controls from the hosting provider, a clear example of the inheritance concept from BC-105 — the customer does not implement data-center physical security, they inherit and document it. The Maintenance (MA) family governs system maintenance, including the risks of remote maintenance and the handling of media during maintenance.",
"These families matter because a system is not secured by technical controls alone — it must be recoverable (CP), have a response capability when prevention fails (IR), be physically protected (PE), and be maintained without introducing risk (MA). Assessors test these families for real capability: a Contingency Plan that has never been tested, or an Incident Response Plan no one has exercised, is a finding — echoing the BC-101 lesson that an untested backup is hope, not a control. Documenting them well means showing tested, exercised, real operational capability, and correctly mapping the inherited ones."],
ex:"An ISSO documents CP-9 (System Backup) with the backup schedule, the immutable offsite copy, and — critically — the results of the last restoration test against the system's RTO, and PE controls by referencing the hosting provider's inherited physical security. Showing a tested backup and correctly mapping inherited physical controls satisfies these families with real, evidenced capability rather than untested paper plans.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, IR, CP, PE, MA families","Incident response, contingency, physical, and maintenance control requirements."],["NIST SP 800-34 Rev.1","Contingency planning that CP-family controls formalize."]],
kc:[
{q:"The Contingency Planning (CP) family makes which BC-101 concept auditable?",o:["Password strength","Recoverability — backups and recovery with defined RTO/RPO","Network segmentation","Phishing defense"],a:1,x:"CP controls (CP-2, CP-9, CP-10) require a tested recovery capability, encoding business continuity and backup as requirements."},
{q:"Physical and Environmental (PE) controls in a cloud-hosted system are usually:",o:["Implemented by the customer from scratch","Inherited common controls from the hosting provider, documented as inherited","Not required","Assessed by the customer's facilities team"],a:1,x:"Data-center physical security is provided by the provider and inherited — a clear application of BC-105's inheritance concept."},
{q:"An Incident Response Plan (IR-8) that has never been exercised is:",o:["Fully compliant","A likely finding — untested plans are the paper-plan equivalent of an untested backup","Exempt from assessment","Better than a tested plan"],a:1,x:"Assessors test for real capability; an unexercised IR or contingency plan is hope, not a demonstrated control, per BC-101."}]},
{t:"Assurance Families: CA, RA, PM, SR",d:"Assessment, risk, program management, and supply chain.",
obj:["Describe the CA, RA, PM, and SR families and their roles","Explain how these families tie the control set to the RMF process","Explain the growing importance of supply chain controls"],
read:["Several families govern the security program and the assurance process itself. The Security Assessment and Authorization (CA) family requires the assessment and authorization activities of the RMF — CA-2 (Control Assessments), CA-5 (Plan of Action and Milestones), CA-7 (Continuous Monitoring) — literally encoding the RMF process from BC-105 as controls. The Risk Assessment (RA) family requires risk assessment and vulnerability scanning — RA-3 (Risk Assessment), RA-5 (Vulnerability Monitoring and Scanning) — connecting to the vulnerability management of IC-202.",
"The Program Management (PM) family is unusual: its controls apply at the organizational program level rather than to individual systems, covering the overall security program, risk strategy, and enterprise architecture — the governance layer from BC-101 expressed as controls. The Supply Chain Risk Management (SR) family, elevated in Rev.5, addresses risks from the supply chain — SR-3 (Supply Chain Controls), SR-11 (Component Authenticity) — reflecting the reality that compromised vendors and components (detailed in AC-304) are a major threat vector.",
"These families close the loop between the control catalog and the framework: CA-5 is the POA&M that tracks findings to closure, CA-7 is the continuous monitoring that sustains authorization (BC-105), and RA-5 is the scanning that feeds both. The SR family's growing prominence reflects the supply-chain attacks that have reshaped the threat landscape. Documenting these well means showing that the assurance machinery actually runs — assessments happen, the POA&M is maintained, scanning occurs, and supply-chain risks are considered — the program-level controls that make the system-level ones trustworthy."],
ex:"An ISSO documents CA-5 with the live POA&M tracking open findings to dated milestones, CA-7 with the continuous monitoring strategy and its metrics, and RA-5 with the scheduled authenticated vulnerability scans and their remediation feed. These show the assurance process is operating, not just declared — the machinery that keeps the system-level controls honest over time.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, CA, RA, PM, SR families","Assessment/authorization, risk, program management, and supply chain controls."],["NIST SP 800-161 Rev.1","Supply chain risk management practices that the SR family formalizes."]],
kc:[
{q:"The CA family (CA-2, CA-5, CA-7) encodes which process as controls?",o:["Password management","The RMF assessment, POA&M, and continuous monitoring activities","Physical security","Network routing"],a:1,x:"CA controls make the assessment, plan-of-action-and-milestones, and continuous-monitoring steps of the RMF into auditable requirements."},
{q:"The Program Management (PM) family is unusual because its controls:",o:["Apply to individual systems","Apply at the organizational program level rather than to a single system","Are never assessed","Concern only firewalls"],a:1,x:"PM controls address the enterprise security program and governance layer, not one system — the organizational context from BC-101."},
{q:"The Supply Chain Risk Management (SR) family was elevated in Rev.5 because:",o:["Supply chains are unimportant","Compromised vendors and components have become a major threat vector","It replaced access control","Scanning was removed"],a:1,x:"Supply-chain attacks reshaped the threat landscape; SR controls address component authenticity and vendor risk (detailed in AC-304)."}]},
{t:"Common Weaknesses in Control Documentation",d:"The rejection patterns that generate findings.",
obj:["Catalog the recurring weaknesses that cause control findings","Recognize each weakness in draft documentation","Apply the corresponding fix to each"],
read:["Most control findings are not exotic — they are the same handful of documentation weaknesses repeated, and knowing them lets you catch them before an assessor does. The recurring rejection patterns, drawn from real accepted-and-rejected packages: future-tense narratives that describe intent rather than implementation; restated control text that answers nothing; vague statements naming no mechanism; undated or unscoped evidence; missing required enhancements; weak or unfilled parameters; and inaccurate statements claiming controls that are not actually configured. Each has a specific, known fix.",
"These weaknesses cluster into three root causes. Some are precision failures (vague, restated, or requirement-echoing statements) fixed by naming the specific mechanism, configuration, and verifier. Some are accuracy failures (statements describing a system as designed rather than built, or claiming unimplemented controls) fixed by documenting reality and opening a POA&M for genuine gaps rather than papering over them. Some are completeness failures (missing enhancements, unfilled parameters, absent evidence) fixed by treating every enhancement, parameter, and evidence requirement as a distinct obligation.",
"The professional discipline is self-review against this known list before submission — reading each statement and asking: is it present-tense and accurate? does it name a mechanism? are all enhancements addressed and parameters filled? is the evidence dated, scoped, and matching? A package that has been scrubbed against these patterns submits clean; one that has not generates a POA&M full of avoidable documentation findings that delay authorization and erode assessor confidence in the whole package. This module is the quality-control checklist that the capstone applies."],
ex:"Reviewing a draft before submission, an ISSO finds three statements in future tense, one restating the catalog, two with unfilled parameters, and one claiming an MFA configuration that was never enabled. They rewrite the first four to present-tense named mechanisms, fill the parameters, and — for the unimplemented MFA — document the actual state and open a POA&M item rather than lying. The scrubbed package submits clean; the un-scrubbed one would have generated seven findings.",
refs:[["CCoE Common Mistakes Library (Training Academy)","The paired failing-and-corrected examples behind most package rejections."],["NIST SP 800-53A Rev.5","Assessment procedures that expose these documentation weaknesses."]],
kc:[
{q:"The correct response to a control that is genuinely not yet implemented is to:",o:["Write a statement claiming it is implemented","Document the actual state accurately and open a POA&M item for the gap","Delete the control","Copy another system's statement"],a:1,x:"Accuracy over cover-up: a real gap is documented and tracked in the POA&M, not concealed with a false statement that will fail assessment."},
{q:"Most control findings stem from:",o:["Novel, unpredictable errors","A recurring handful of documentation weaknesses — future tense, restated text, vague statements, weak evidence","Assessor bias","The control catalog itself"],a:1,x:"The rejection patterns are well-known and repetitive; self-review against them catches most findings before submission."},
{q:"Self-reviewing a package against the known weakness patterns before submission primarily:",o:["Wastes time","Catches avoidable documentation findings, so the package submits clean and preserves assessor confidence","Replaces the assessment","Is only for new ISSOs"],a:1,x:"Scrubbing against the known patterns prevents the avoidable findings that delay authorization and erode trust in the package."}]},
{t:"Control Enhancements and Parameters in Depth",d:"Getting the details right where audits are won or lost.",
obj:["Explain how enhancements raise assurance and where they apply","Explain the importance of correctly assigned parameters","Identify common enhancement and parameter mistakes"],
read:["Enhancements and parameters are where control implementation succeeds or fails in detail. A baseline may require not just AC-2 but specific enhancements (AC-2(1) automated account management, AC-2(3) disable inactive accounts, AC-2(12) monitor for atypical usage), each adding a distinct, testable requirement. Implementers who address the base control but overlook the required enhancements produce incomplete implementations that fail assessment — the enhancement was in the baseline and was not satisfied.",
"Parameters, as established in module one, must carry the organization's actual values, and getting them right is substantive. A parameter set too weakly (a 90-day inactive-account threshold when policy says 35, a session timeout of hours) undermines the control even though it is technically populated; a parameter left as an unfilled placeholder is an obvious finding. Parameters should trace to organizational policy and risk tolerance, and the same parameter often appears across related controls, requiring consistency (the account-inactivity period, the audit-review frequency).",
"The practical discipline is to treat every applicable enhancement and every parameter as a distinct requirement to satisfy and document, not detail to gloss. Assessors specifically check enhancements and parameter values because that is where under-implementation hides — a system can claim to satisfy AC-2 while missing three required enhancements and setting its parameters permissively. This attention to detail is what separates a package that passes cleanly from one that generates a list of findings, and it is why the next modules focus on writing and evidencing controls precisely."],
ex:"An assessor reviews AC-2 and finds the base control implemented but enhancement AC-2(3) — automatically disabling inactive accounts — not configured, and the inactivity parameter left blank. The base statement looked complete, but the missing required enhancement and unfilled parameter are findings. Addressing each enhancement and parameter as its own requirement would have prevented them.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, enhancement structure","How enhancements add distinct, testable requirements to base controls."],["NIST SP 800-53A Rev.5","Assessment procedures showing how enhancements and parameters are tested."]],
kc:[
{q:"A common implementation failure with control enhancements is:",o:["Implementing too many","Satisfying the base control but overlooking required enhancements that are also in the baseline","Using the wrong prefix","Documenting them twice"],a:1,x:"Enhancements are distinct required elements; addressing only the base control leaves the enhancements unsatisfied — a finding."},
{q:"A parameter set to a weak value (e.g., a 90-day timeout when policy requires 15 minutes) is:",o:["Fully compliant","Technically populated but undermines the control — assessors check parameter values, not just presence","Not assessed","Always acceptable"],a:1,x:"Weak parameters gut otherwise-selected controls; values must trace to policy and risk tolerance, and assessors verify them."},
{q:"Why do assessors specifically scrutinize enhancements and parameters?",o:["They are decorative","That is where under-implementation hides — a control can appear satisfied while missing enhancements or set permissively","They are optional","They never change"],a:1,x:"Detail-level gaps in enhancements and parameters are where incomplete implementations conceal themselves, so assessors target them."}]},
{t:"Writing Control Implementation Statements",d:"Present tense, named mechanisms, named verifiers.",
obj:["State the properties of a strong implementation statement","Identify and fix weak statement patterns","Write statements that are specific, verifiable, and accurate"],
read:["A control implementation statement answers one question: how does this system satisfy this control? A strong statement is specific (names the actual mechanism — the identity provider, the firewall model, the configuration), present-tense (describes what the system does now, not what it will do), and verifiable (an assessor can check it against the system). It says what this system does, not what the control requires. This is the single most important documentation skill an ISSO has, because the SSP is only as good as its statements.",
"The weak patterns, catalogued across this program, are predictable: future-tense statements (will be configured) describe intent, not implementation, and are unassessable; copy-pasted control text restates the requirement instead of answering it; vague statements (uses strong security) name no mechanism and cannot be verified; and inaccurate statements (claiming controls that are not actually configured) fail the moment the assessor checks. Each weak pattern is a finding waiting to happen, and recognizing them in your own writing is how you avoid them.",
"The fix is a repeatable structure: state what the control accomplishes on this system, the specific mechanism that accomplishes it, how it is configured (with the parameter value), and who or what verifies it. This is the CCoE control-implementation-frame discipline — the difference between the system controls access (unassessable) and access is enforced by [named RBAC platform] using roles defined per the access-control policy, with MFA required via [named IdP], reviewed quarterly by system owners. The latter names mechanism, configuration, and verifier, and survives assessment."],
ex:"An ISSO rewrites a weak statement — 'the system will implement audit logging' — into a strong one: 'the system forwards Windows Security and Sysmon events to the enterprise SIEM in real time; audit content includes user, event, timestamp, source, and outcome per AU-3; logs are reviewed daily by the SOC and retained 12 months.' Present-tense, mechanism-named, parameter-filled, verifier-named — the rewrite is exactly what passes assessment.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-18","Guidance on documenting control implementation in the SSP."],["CCoE Control Implementation Statement Frames (Templates library)","Sentence frames for what/how/who/how-often/evidence."]],
kc:[
{q:"A strong control implementation statement is characterized by:",o:["Restating the control requirement","Being specific, present-tense, and verifiable — naming the actual mechanism and configuration","Using future tense","Being as vague as possible"],a:1,x:"It says what this system does now, with a named mechanism an assessor can verify — not a restatement or a promise."},
{q:"A future-tense statement like 'MFA will be configured' fails because it:",o:["Is too long","Describes intent, not current implementation, and is therefore unassessable","Uses the wrong font","Names a mechanism"],a:1,x:"Assessors test what exists now; future-tense describes a plan, not an implemented control — a classic finding."},
{q:"Copy-pasting the control catalog text as the implementation statement is weak because it:",o:["Is efficient","Restates the requirement instead of describing how this specific system satisfies it","Is always accurate","Names the verifier"],a:1,x:"The statement must answer how this system meets the control; restating the requirement provides nothing assessable."}]},
{t:"Evidence and Artifacts for Controls",d:"Proving implementation, not just asserting it.",
obj:["Explain why controls require supporting evidence","Describe qualities of good evidence and common evidence failures","Connect evidence to the assessment methods"],
read:["A control implementation statement claims the system satisfies a control; evidence proves it. Assessors verify controls through examination (documents, configurations, screenshots), interview (asking personnel), and testing (directly checking the system) — the three methods from BC-107. Good evidence supports these: a configuration export showing MFA enforced, a screenshot of the account-review record, a policy document, a test result. Without evidence, a statement is an unverified assertion, and assessment is exactly the process of demanding proof for each claim.",
"Good evidence has qualities this program emphasizes repeatedly: it is dated, scoped, and attributable (a screenshot with a timestamp, hostname, and collection note, not an anonymous undated image), it matches the implementation statement (evidence for what the statement claims, not something adjacent), and it is current (reflecting the system as it is now). The common evidence failures — undated screenshots that could be any system at any time, evidence that does not actually demonstrate the control, stale evidence from a prior configuration — are precisely the rejection patterns the CCoE common-mistakes library catalogs.",
"Assembling evidence is not a last-minute assessment scramble but an ongoing discipline: as controls are implemented, their evidence is collected and organized by control, so that at assessment time the package presents each statement with its supporting proof. This connects to the assessment-readiness practice (inspection readiness is a daily condition, not a two-week panic) and to continuous monitoring, where evidence is continuously refreshed. A strong package pairs every material control statement with dated, scoped, matching evidence — the difference between a claim and a demonstrated fact."],
ex:"For the MFA control statement, the ISSO provides a dated configuration export from the identity provider showing MFA enforced for the privileged group, annotated with the hostname and collection date. The evidence matches the claim, is timestamped and scoped, and is current — it demonstrates the control rather than merely asserting it, exactly what the assessor needs to verify without a finding.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53A Rev.5","Assessment procedures and the evidence each control's assessment expects."],["CCoE POA&M Entry & Closure Evidence Forms (Templates library)","Standardized evidence capture aligned to assessment."]],
kc:[
{q:"Evidence for a control should be:",o:["Undated and generic for flexibility","Dated, scoped, attributable, and matching the specific implementation statement","Only a verbal assurance","From any similar system"],a:1,x:"Good evidence is timestamped, scoped to the system, and demonstrates the exact claim — undated generic artifacts are a rejection pattern."},
{q:"Assessors verify controls through which three methods?",o:["Scan, patch, report","Examine, Interview, Test","Plan, do, check","Detect, contain, recover"],a:1,x:"Examination of artifacts, interviewing personnel, and testing the system are the three assessment methods evidence must support."},
{q:"Collecting control evidence should be:",o:["A last-minute scramble before assessment","An ongoing discipline, organized by control, so the package is always assessment-ready","Skipped until findings appear","Done only once ever"],a:1,x:"Readiness is a continuous condition; evidence collected and organized as controls are implemented avoids the assessment-time panic."}]},
{t:"Controls Documentation Capstone",d:"Producing an audit-ready control package for a system.",
obj:["Synthesize catalog navigation, statement writing, and evidencing into a package","Recognize and remediate weak control documentation","Produce implementation statements that survive assessment"],
read:["This module assembles the course into the ISSO's core deliverable: an audit-ready set of control implementation statements with evidence. The workflow: for each control in the tailored baseline (from BC-105), determine whether it is system-specific, inherited, or hybrid; write a specific, present-tense, verifiable implementation statement naming the mechanism, configuration, and verifier; address every required enhancement and fill every parameter with the actual value; and pair the statement with dated, scoped, matching evidence. Done across the baseline, this produces the SSP body that the assessment tests and the AO relies on.",
"The synthesis draws every module together: catalog structure and families to navigate the controls, the technical families (AC, AU, CM, IA, SC, SI) to understand what each requires, enhancement and parameter discipline for the details, statement-writing craft for the prose, and evidence discipline for the proof. The recurring failure modes — future tense, restated requirements, missing enhancements, weak parameters, undated evidence — are the checklist of what to avoid, and recognizing them in a draft is the quality-control skill this course exists to build.",
"The measure of success is assessment survival: a package whose statements are specific and accurate, whose enhancements and parameters are complete, and whose evidence is current and matching passes cleanly; one riddled with the weak patterns generates a POA&M full of documentation findings before a single technical weakness is even considered. The graded deliverable — audit-ready implementation statements for ten core AC and AU controls — is exactly this, and its quality is judged by whether an assessor could verify each statement against the system without a finding.",],
ex:"An ISSO reviews a colleague's draft AC-2 statement, catches that it restates the control in future tense and omits AC-2(3), rewrites it to name the identity-governance mechanism in present tense with the inactivity parameter filled, and attaches a dated configuration export. The remediated statement now names mechanism, configuration, enhancement, and verifier with matching evidence — transformed from a certain finding into a clean pass.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-18 and SP 800-53A Rev.5","Documenting and assessing controls — the SSP and the assessment procedures together."],["CCoE Control Implementation Statement Frames and Gold Standard (Templates library)","Worked examples of statements that pass the first time."]],
kc:[
{q:"An audit-ready control statement pairs a specific, present-tense description with:",o:["A promise of future work","Dated, scoped evidence that matches and demonstrates the claim","No evidence","The control catalog text"],a:1,x:"Statement plus matching, current evidence is what an assessor verifies; a claim without proof is an unverified assertion."},
{q:"Before technical weaknesses are even considered, a package riddled with weak documentation patterns will:",o:["Pass automatically","Generate a POA&M full of documentation findings (future tense, missing enhancements, undated evidence)","Be exempt from assessment","Improve the categorization"],a:1,x:"Documentation quality is assessed directly; the recurring weak patterns become findings regardless of the underlying technical posture."},
{q:"The success measure for the control documentation deliverable is:",o:["Length of the statements","Whether an assessor could verify each statement against the system without a finding","Number of controls restated","Use of future tense"],a:1,x:"Assessment survival — specific, accurate, complete, evidenced statements — is the standard the deliverable is judged against."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: An assessor flags AC-2 because inactive accounts are not auto-disabled and the inactivity parameter is blank. The lesson is:",o:["AC-2 is optional","Required enhancements (AC-2(3)) and parameters are distinct obligations that must each be satisfied and documented","The base control is enough","Parameters do not matter"],a:1,x:"Enhancements and parameters are separately assessed; satisfying only the base control while missing them produces findings."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A statement reads 'the system will implement audit logging.' An assessor will:",o:["Accept it as compliant","Flag it — future tense describes intent, not an implemented, verifiable control","Ignore logging controls","Raise the categorization"],a:1,x:"Assessors test what exists now; future-tense statements are unassessable and are a classic documentation finding."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Evidence for an MFA control is an undated screenshot with no hostname. The problem is:",o:["Screenshots are never allowed","It could be any system at any time — evidence must be dated, scoped, and attributable to demonstrate the claim","MFA cannot be evidenced","It is perfectly fine"],a:1,x:"Undated, unscoped evidence is a rejection pattern; good evidence carries timestamp, hostname, and collection context matching the statement."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A scanner flags deficiencies in CM-6 and SI-2. An ISSO fluent in the families knows these concern:",o:["Physical security and privacy","Configuration settings (secure baseline) and flaw remediation (patching)","Access control and audit","Contingency and maintenance"],a:1,x:"CM-6 is secure configuration settings and SI-2 is flaw remediation — family fluency turns identifiers into an actionable interpretation."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A High system uses strong encryption but with an unvalidated cryptographic module. Under SC-13 this is:",o:["Fully compliant","A finding — federal systems require FIPS-validated modules, not merely strong encryption","Not assessed","Better than validated crypto"],a:1,x:"SC-13 requires validated modules; strong encryption implemented with an unvalidated or misconfigured module fails the control."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An SI-4 assessment finds a monitoring tool installed but no alerting or response process. The result:",o:["Compliant, since the tool exists","A finding — SI-4 requires functioning monitoring with coverage, alerting, and response, not mere tool presence","SI-4 is optional","Only the tool matters"],a:1,x:"SI-4 mandates effective monitoring; an unused tool without alerting or response does not satisfy the detection requirement."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A control statement copy-pastes the 800-53 catalog text for AC-6. This will be flagged because it:",o:["Is too detailed","Restates the requirement instead of describing how this specific system enforces least privilege","Names too many mechanisms","Uses present tense"],a:1,x:"The statement must answer how this system satisfies the control; restating the catalog provides nothing verifiable."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A CM-3 (change control) gap allowed undocumented changes that drifted the system from its SSP. The consequence is:",o:["Improved security","Baseline drift that silently invalidates the authorization and generates findings when inspectors compare SSP to reality","Faster operations with no risk","A lower categorization"],a:1,x:"Uncontrolled change causes the paper-mirroring divergence between SSP and system that undermines the authorization."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An ISSO documents AU-6 by noting logs are collected but cannot show they are reviewed. The assessment risk is:",o:["None","A finding — AU-6 requires actual review, analysis, and reporting; unreviewed logs do not satisfy the control","Logs are exempt","Only retention matters"],a:1,x:"AU-6 requires demonstrated review; collection alone (the ignored-console failure) does not meet the control."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A package is judged assessment-ready. The correct standard for that judgment is:",o:["It is long and thorough-looking","An assessor could verify each specific, present-tense statement against the system with dated matching evidence, without a finding","It restates every control","It uses future tense to show planning"],a:1,x:"Readiness means verifiable statements plus matching current evidence — the standard that separates a clean pass from a findings list."}
]};
